          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Nikki\",\"addressee\":\"Keith\",\"anchor_quote\":\"I love you\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows a present loving emotional state toward Keith. The narration further frames the utterance as an honest admission rather than a hypothetical or merely conventional phrase.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake a defined obligation, promise continued conduct, or establish agreed relationship terms. Keith reciprocates the avowal, but that uptake does not convert Nikki's words into a commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Keith stilled, then smiled. “I love you too,” he said, his voice steady and sincere.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The utterance is represented not only as an assertion but also as an affect-driven, relatively unplanned verbal outpouring: it occurs suddenly and escapes before Nikki can inhibit it. Its framing as an admission and honest avowal keeps T dominant, so the expressive-discharge support is substantial rather than exclusive.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"“I love you,” she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No core force beyond the loving avowal and its spontaneous affective discharge is evident. In particular, the expression is not explicitly a request, command, question, or declaration that changes their formal relationship status.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have had sex and are lying together in bed, Nikki turns to Keith with her “heart full” and spontaneously tells him that she loves him. Keith pauses, smiles, and reciprocates the avowal.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Nikki undertakes no specific obligation in the focal expression. Keith accepts and reciprocates the emotional avowal by saying, “I love you too,” but neither speaker states definite relationship terms or a promise about future conduct here.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognise the supplied passage as a particular known work.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports a sincere present avowal and spontaneous affective expression, but it does not establish the durability of Nikki's feelings, any precise future intention, or an agreed ongoing relationship commitment.\"}"
        }
